Two voice memos, both while listening to [[Scratching the Surface]] episode 293, where [[Kathleen Sleboda]] interviews [[Jarrett Fuller]] about his book [[Graphic Designers After Graphic Design]]. https://scratchingthesurface.fm/293-graphic-designers-after-graphic-design

!! 8:03am

I was only four minutes into your podcast episode about your book, but I was really intrigued by this idea that you said, well, what if graphic design doesn't really exist anymore? Maybe the question is, did graphic design ever really exist? There's just been this tiny window where the things that we call graphic design [were made by] a group of people that didn't make anything else other than graphic design, or were makers of it without being attached to the production of it exactly. It's sort of an output of modernist art and design curriculum post [[BauHaus]] more than anything else. Maybe post William Morris arts and crafts movement.

But this idea that you're not a printer. You're not any other sort of craftsperson. You're not an engraver. You're a graphic designer. And all of the first graphic designers that we think of in graphic design history were also in the [[if you can design one thing, you can design anything|If you can Design one thing, you can design anything]] mode. [[László Moholy-Nagy]] [[design is an attitude|Design as an Attitude]] mode. And it's really only been, what, 80 years tops, where there's people just doing the visual design and nothing else in the process. Probably post-World War II. Anyway, I'm digressing.

I think what we're really talking about when we talk about graphic design, though, is [[Semiotics]], right? It's purely: how do I communicate meaning and emotion to someone? And what's the optimal mode for that? And this idea of signaling and using specific kinds of substrates to signal ([[Signs Signaling on Substrates]]) is what [?]. Because even a product designer, if you're making objects, you're still trying to signal something to a person. The shapes, the colors, the materials you use are meant to mean something, not just be nice. I mean, being nice means something. Again, I digress.

I'm really hung up on: what I'm doing as a graphic designer is purposefully engaging signs and meaning. That's the first thing I'm doing. And it doesn't matter what medium or tool I use to do that. The communicative layer is why I'm graphic designing and not something else. Or perhaps I'm just designing and it doesn't matter if there's an adjective in front of it.

I think of that in all my projects, either making an app, or my goofy plastic recycling thing, or this new chair project I'm working on. To me, those are graphic design projects. I don't care about them as a chair-making project. That is a fun part of it, but the chairs are meant to be flags or communicative devices that ask a question about [[what is a chair|Chair-ness]], or [convey], through being inspired by this object, how you use the chair. The chair is just the delivery method for the feeling or the concept I'm trying to deliver. Maybe this is because I'm really ultimately a [[conceptual artist|¶ on Conceptual Art Questions]] at heart, not really a graphic designer.

!! 8:40am

[Whisper lost the first few seconds.] I already have more ideas. You're talking about the net, the web, the interconnections of these designers. I'd love to help work on that as some sort of dynamic web thing. Maybe with some goofy open source tool no one else would have thought of to use, like [[TiddlyWiki]], to keep it novel and interesting and a little more [[design commons|New Design Commons]] oriented. Or exhibition-wise: MICA would love to be a place that showed something like that, in the design galleries on the third and fourth floor, or on the exhibitions calendar for Fox or Lazarus.

[The interest] is in knowing about things and thinking about interconnections, not necessarily doing client work.

